esults it's the last year of  our life and over the next decade  there'll be about 25 more people  reaching the last year of their life so  you've got population aging you've got  repent up demands of covid you've got  unfortunately the fact that as a country  we have high levels of poverty poverty  is stroke is is strongly connected to  ill health is your last interview  vividly demonstrated so we've got a lot  of demand but then when it comes to  capacity we had 10 years of austerity  when growth in Health Service spending  was around half of what most economists  say that we need that meant we went into  covert there was a research last week  that showed this about about 40 billion  pounds less have been invested in the UK  in comparison to EU countries in that  decade leading up to Cobalt so we go  into covid with over a hundred thousand  vacancies with a crumbling estate lack  of investment in in psychiat and covet  hits us extremely hard the effects that  covert had in terms of for example  waiting lists was much worse in the UK  because we just didn't have the extra  capacity so we've come out of that and  this is the issue this gap between  demand and capacity now you know we  welcome the fact the secretary of state  has got more money out of the treasury  to deal with the impact of inflation  it's not as much as we need not as much  as we want but we're grateful for  anything that we can get  but what we really need now is  consistent spending over the next five  or ten years and we've got to address  these two fundamental issues 130 000  vacancies in hell so it's 160 000 in  Social and a 10 billion pound backlog in  terms of the estate and it's only when  we fundamentally address those capacity  issues that we're going to get the  Health Service that we need and that we  want  it's interesting for you to talk though  about you know the health of the Health  Service going into uh covid because of  course you know minister is very keen to  talk about the impact of the pandemic  exclusively  um now you're talking about the  challenges of  um extra Demand with the Aging  population and how much more Health  Service we consume in the later years of  Our Lives  now the NHS of course is consuming an  increasing amount of public spending I  think it's a almost 200 billion pounds a  year 20 of public spending continuing to  rise  is the problem that actually money isn't  the only solution that we need reform as  well  I I do think we can do things better I  think we can be more productive but the  problem is that some of the ways in  which we're not productive are a  reflection of that lack of investment so  let me give you a couple of examples we  spend a lot of money on agency staff and  that's a very expensive way of employing  people but that's because we don't have  that Workforce strategy so one of the  things I really welcome about the Autumn  statement is a commitment by the  government to have a proper medium-term  Workforce strategy take another example  almost every hospital in England in  Wales in Northern Ireland has got Wards  full of people who could go home who  should go home hospitals are not a good  place for me to be if you don't need to  be there you get in a deconditioned your  your situation tends to kind of  deteriorate now it costs more to have  people in hospitals than it does to have  them in Care Homes and a lot more than  it does to have them getting domiciliary  care but we don't have that social Care  staff the lack of investment in capital  means that services like Primary Care  aren't as efficient as they as they  ought to be so we can be more productive  but it's not about kind of you know  cutting away at bureaucracy actually we  have fewer managers in the Health  Service than than most other kind of  systems large large systems like ours  it's about that medium-term investment  in a Better Health Service a Health  Service for example that could be better  at diagnostic so we we find diseases uh  earlier so we all want a more productive  Health Service we all want a Better  Health Service and to be fair to the  Secretary of State I think many of  things he's talking about whether it's  data I.T evolving power are in the right  direction  but it's going to take time you know we  are not in the situation we want to be  in the demand is growing and it is going  to take time for us to rebuild the  health  I'm talking about social care were you  disappointed that there wasn't more  about social care in the Autumn  statement  well there's some more money for social  care I mean the government faced a  difficult Choice which is that most of  the extra money that was dedicated to  social care and the Austria's Autumn  statement was going towards the cap on  social care costs now the government has  put that off for two years that's an  unpopular thing to do but by doing that  there's more money which can go into the  front line and I I think on balance  given that resources are limited that  was probably the right thing to do  because when you've got a hundred and  seventy thousand 160 000 vacancies in  Social care the situation just grinds to  a whole  and if you talk to anyone in local  government who's trying to recruit  people into social care the fact is that  you know cost of living crisis is hard  for everyone but the private sector in  the end can pass on extra costs it can  raise wages and I I speak to people  every day who work in Health and Social  care and they talk about staff leaving  to go and work in warehouses to go and  work in retail something which  intensifies in Crystal so that situation  is deteriorating so in a perfect world  there'd be more money for social care in  the front line and we'd be able to carry  out the reforms that Boris Johnson  unveiled some time ago but given the  choices the really important thing is to  get that money to the front line and and  two for example get those people out of  Hospital who don't need to be the